          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For most pet owners the answer is a <strong class="text-white">motorised dog treadmill with
            solid side panels and a genuinely low minimum speed</strong>, sized on belt length
            rather than on the space you have. Slatmills and carpet mills are excellent for canine
            athletes and overkill for everyone else.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whatever you buy, the two rules do not bend: the introduction takes a fortnight and cannot
            be hurried, and the dog is never left on it alone. Talk to your vet before you start,
            and keep the
            <a href="/how-to-vacuum-treadmill-motor-compartment/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">motor compartment clear of hair</a>
            — on a dog machine that is the maintenance job that decides how long it lasts.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
